<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarbela Dam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1976">1976</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tarbela Dam is an earth-fill dam on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-frontier-province">Khyber Pakhtunkhwa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, completed in 1976 as part of the works that followed the Indus Waters Treaty. One of the largest dams of its kind in the world, it provides irrigation water and a large share of Pakistan's hydroelectric power. Its reservoir flooded many villages, including the old seat of the Amb state at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Darband</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarbela-dam.jpg" alt="Tarbela Dam" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Duncan, U.S. Marine Corps · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarbela_Dam_during_the_2010_floods.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
